**Summary**: The set $\{(\aleph_{\omega_1})^\kappa : 1 \leq \kappa < \aleph_{\omega_1}\}$ takes exactly **two** distinct values:
- $\aleph_{\omega_1}$ when $1 \leq \kappa < \omega_1$ (Case 1, via bounded-range formula),
- $\prod_{\alpha < \omega_1} \aleph_\alpha$ when $\kappa \geq \omega_1$ (Case 2, via level-function decomposition + Key Claim that all unbounded level functions yield the same product).

The two values are distinct by König's theorem ($\sum_\alpha \aleph_\alpha < \prod_\alpha \aleph_{\alpha+1} \leq \prod_\alpha \aleph_\alpha$).

